# Browse household guides from A to Z

Use an A to Z when people know the name of the guide they want but not where it sits in the navigation. These examples are local guidance. They do not add a Home Assistant screen or perform a device action.

## When to use it

Use it for one large collection on one subject, such as appliance guides. If the collection is small, use a short list under meaningful headings instead of an almost empty alphabet. Do not sort live controls by entity ID: an A to Z of guides is not an entity browser.

## Keep one subject

Name the subject in the title. “Appliance guides A to Z” works better than a mixed list of meters, household members, scenes and account settings. Use familiar names in link text, and add an entity ID only where it helps. For example, write “Vacuum cleaner”, not `vacuum.hallway`.

<%= example('nhs-az-household-guides') %>

## Send every alias to one guide

A household may say “Hoover” where the guide says “Vacuum cleaner”. List the alias in the index, but link it to the one maintained guide so the instructions cannot diverge. Do not show another household's private device names.

<%= example('nhs-az-aliases') %>

## Handle letters with no entries

Make only letters with entries interactive. If a bookmarked letter has no guides, say the index has no matching guide and link back to the full collection. Do not show a dead link, and do not suggest that the integration has no devices.

<%= example('nhs-az-empty') %>

## Build it accessibly

- Put “A to Z” in the title and give the letter navigation an accessible name.
- Use a heading for each letter and real anchors, so keyboard navigation, browser history and bookmarks work.
- Keep letter links large enough to select and let them wrap at 320 CSS pixels.
- Do not use a sticky alphabet bar that covers focused content when the page is zoomed.
- Offer search as well, not instead, unless research shows people do not need the index.

Link text follows [Write effective links](../content/writing-guidelines/add-links.html). Test with the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html).

## Keep it separate from device state

Selecting a letter or a guide only moves the person to help. It must never call a service or toggle a device. Keep the guide catalogue apart from the current entity registry, so a disconnected integration does not remove its recovery instructions.

## Maintain it

Review aliases when a guide is renamed and keep useful old routes. Sort consistently for the chosen language. The examples use synthetic guides and navigate only within the preview.
